Z||000000||KRMFiero||03-03-2001||08:23 PM||||Questions for my 85, I4, 5 speed, white fiero.<P>Okay, I just was looking at my trottle cable cause it was sticking, I noticed there is a area where all the covering around the cable is melted off, so i have to get a new cable (Where is the best place to buy, how hard is it to replace, any advice)<P>I was cheaking my tail lights, My reverse lights do not work (I have a 5 speed), and my brake lights do not always work. (Any ideas, what to cheak for, is there any connections i should cheak, how do i cheak the bulbs?)<P>You have to press the clutch all the way to the floor is that normal in fieros (My other fiero is Auto so i dont know) The clutch seems to work fine but i dont like having to put it to the floor for it to do something<P>My horn, It dont work... I press the botton i dont hear the relay do anything (i tryed swaping relays with my 84 and there is no difference, I have the Steering wheel with the horn bottons on eather side) Any Ideas? what do i cheak for?<P>My Power windows, my passanger side goes down/up VERY slow. Any idea? my drivers side clicks in the door but nothing happins (what do i look for, any ideas)<P>My battery tray is completl rotted, battery is being supposted by duct tape and bungie cords, where can i get a replacement tray or should i move it to the front, who has best prices?<P>My belt (I think) sqeeks real bad when it is reved to about 3K, how do i fix it?<P>Also what is the normal operating tempature of the duke? mine runs at about 220*, thats after like a hour at about 2500 rpm<P>Help me!<BR>Thanks<BR>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<P>  <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||03-03-2001||09:01 PM||||SOME answers<P>the window that does work prob need interior door panel taken off, everything inside cleaned and lubed. It cakes up and dries out.<BR>the drivers door motor prob has a stripped gear, so youll have to replace the motor inside the door.<P>squeeling is prob a loose belt, just loosen the one bolt on alternator and replace the belt. It doesnt cost that much and prob needs replaced anyway. when you tighten it use a long prybar to hold it tite while you tighten the bolt on alternator. <P>Every Fiero ive driven, the clutch has to go all the way to the floor, so I wouldnt worry unless its grinding changing gears.<P>brake lites is prob not bulbs since they work sometimes, id say if after u check the bulbs to see if there all good, its the white plastic switch at the top of the brake pedal. Its ez to replace. Make sure you do the right one, if you have cruise control theres 2. you can check bulbs by turning on key and turning on turn signals. the brake lites use the same filiment. If they work with signals-theyll work for brakes.<P>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-03-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Chance||03-05-2001||02:01 AM||||     Sometimes, there is nothing I like better then on a cool summer evening, getting my flood light out, and go tweak out on all the piddly things on my Fiero for 6 or so hours!<P>  Since my father was the original MacGuyver and cheap to boot, I learned what can get me by till I can get it done properly with more time and money.  <P> On the belt, I would get some belt stop squeak spray and give it a blast or two.  An even cheaper temporary fix would be pour a little sticky soda on it. (believe it,it works)<P> Slow power window? more then likely the bush/comunicator(sp) needs cleaned.  If you don't want to tear apart the motor, you can try spraying it out with electric contact cleaner.  <P> Battery Tray, I got one a few years ago (plastic) from Wal-Mart for a couple bucks for another vehicle I had... but it fits real nice in Fiero.  Hopefully, there is enough metal around to support it. Even cheaper? good ol'piece of board.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P> On the Cable, I would get some Teflon lube, or WD-40 and spray into the exposed section, and work it till it wasn't sticking anymore (and spray around where the cable connects to the intake) then slit a McDonalds straw, put it over the broke area, and wrap with tape...Voila! <P> I told my brother how to fix HIS Fiero shift cable with the straw/tape and my Favorite fix it tool... "Plastic Weld" (that 2 suringed epoxy) ||||reg||3
Z||000003||Carrolles||03-05-2001||08:18 AM||||You can still get both parts of the battery tray at your Pontiac dealer.  I recently replaced mine for around $25 US for both.||||reg||1
